For a long time, people believed termite mounds kept the nest at a perfectly steady temperature, like a building with [[@air-conditioning|air conditioning]]. Careful measurements showed it is more complicated. Some nests stay fairly steady, while others warm and cool with the seasons.

In 2015, scientists studied mounds of an Indian termite near [[@bengaluru|Bengaluru]]. They placed [[sensor|sensors]] inside and found that the mounds have ridges, called flutes, which heat and cool faster than the centre. In the day, warm air rises through the flutes and sinks down the middle. At night, the flow turns around. The mounds were using the daily [[rhythm|rhythm]] of heat and cold to breathe.

{{story:skyscrapers}}

The pairing rule makes copying easy. When a cell divides, the ladder unzips down the middle. Each half then builds a new partner, putting a T opposite every A and a G opposite every C. You end up with two identical ladders, one for each new cell.

The copying is extremely accurate, but now and then a letter changes. Most changes do nothing. A few cause illness, and a very few turn out to be useful. Over many generations, these small changes are how living things [[evolve|evolve]].

{{story:genes-family}}

Train wheels and rails are both made of hard steel, and they touch over an area about the size of a small coin. There is very little rolling [[@friction|friction]], so a train needs far less energy to move a tonne of cargo than a truck does. One locomotive can pull a goods train more than a kilometre long.

Low [[friction|friction]] has a downside. Steel wheels can slip on wet rails or fallen leaves, and trains take a long time to stop. That's why railway signals warn drivers far ahead, and why level crossings have gates. A heavy goods train moving at full speed can need a kilometre or more to come to a stop.

{{story:steam-engine}}

In the early 1980s, a team led by the artist J. Swaminathan was looking for village artists for a new arts centre, Bharat Bhavan in Bhopal. In Patangarh they met Jangarh Singh Shyam, a young Pardhan Gond who had been painting on the walls of village homes.

They gave him paper, canvas and modern paints. Jangarh took the wall patterns and turned them into large, dazzling pictures of gods, animals and forests. His work was soon shown in galleries in India and abroad. He also trained relatives and neighbours, and a whole school of artists grew around him. Jangarh died young, in 2001, but his style lives on in dozens of painters.

{{story:picture-books}}

Adult men usually have longer, thicker vocal folds than women, so their voices are lower. Children's folds are shorter still. During the teenage years, a boy's larynx grows quickly and his folds get longer, so [[@body-voice-change|his voice drops]], sometimes cracking along the way. Girls' voices change too, but less.

The sound from the folds is only a thin buzz. Your throat, mouth and nose shape it into a rich voice, like the body of a guitar shaping a string's sound. Moving your tongue and lips changes the vowels.

{{story:voice}}

Some government work is easy to spot: a policeman directing traffic, a government school, a passport office. Some is hidden. Governments test medicines, check that food is safe, forecast the weather and decide what time it is officially.

Governments also run huge services. [[@indian-railways|Indian Railways]] carries crores of passengers every day and is owned by the government. The [[@mid-day-meal|mid-day meal]] scheme serves a hot lunch to more than ten crore schoolchildren in government schools.

{{story:democracy-athens}}

In Scratch, the free coding tool made at MIT, the stage is a grid 480 steps wide and 360 high. The centre is (0, 0). Move right and x goes up. Move up and y goes up. This is the same [[@coordinates|coordinate]] grid from maths class, with negative numbers for left and down.

To make a sprite walk, you add to its x. To make it jump, you add to its y, then subtract a bit more each moment to pull it back down. That is a simple model of [[gravity|gravity]]. Many game makers start exactly this way before learning the physics formulas behind real-looking motion.

{{story:algorithms}}

Some calendars follow the [[@moon-phases|Moon]]. A lunar month, from one new moon to the next, is about 29.5 days. Twelve of them make about 354 days, shorter than a solar year.

The Islamic year is purely lunar, so its New Year, like [[@eid|Eid]], moves about 11 days earlier each year. The Chinese calendar is lunisolar: it follows the Moon but adds an extra month every few years to keep pace with the [[@seasons-why|seasons]]. That's why Lunar New Year always lands between late January and mid-February.

{{story:moon-phases}}

If someone burns their [[@skin|skin]] on a hot pan or with hot water, cool it under cool running tap water for about 20 minutes. That may feel like a long time, but it stops the heat from going deeper. Take off rings or tight clothes near the burn, unless they're stuck to the skin.

In many homes in India and elsewhere, people reach for toothpaste, butter or ice. These can make burns worse. Large burns, burns on the face or hands, and any burn on a young child need medical help.

{{story:nobel-peace}}

The rules of inheritance were first worked out by Gregor Mendel, a monk in what is now the Czech Republic. Between 1856 and 1863, he grew thousands of pea plants in his [[monastery|monastery]] garden, carefully crossing tall plants with short ones and yellow peas with green ones.

He noticed that some traits disappeared in one generation and came back in the next. He reasoned that each plant carried two factors for each trait, one of which could hide the other. He published his work in 1866, and almost nobody noticed. Only in 1900 did other scientists rediscover it. Those factors are what we now call genes. Peas, like the [[@pulses-dal|dal]] on your plate, are pulses.

{{story:dna}}

Much of your height comes from your [[@genes-family|genes]], the instructions you inherited from your parents. Tall parents tend to have tall children, though not always. Scientists have found hundreds of [[gene|genes]] that each nudge height a tiny bit.

But genes aren't everything. Children need enough food, especially [[protein|protein]], and enough sleep, and they need to stay mostly free of long illnesses. Across the last hundred years, average heights have gone up in many countries as diets and health care improved. Children today in India, the USA and most of the world are, on average, taller than their great-grandparents were at the same age.

{{story:astronaut-life}}

For a long time, many people kept mental health problems secret. That has started to change, partly because famous people have spoken openly. At the Tokyo Olympics in 2021, the American gymnast [[@simone-biles|Simone Biles]] withdrew from several events. She said her mind and body were not in sync, which is dangerous when you are twisting high in the air. Many people praised her for putting her health first. She returned to win a bronze medal on the balance beam a few days later.

In India, the film actor Deepika Padukone talked publicly in 2015 about her depression and later set up a foundation to fight the shame around it. Stories like these help others realise they are not alone.

{{story:simone-biles}}

A phone is packed with useful materials. Copper wires, gold on the [[circuit|circuit]] board connectors, silver, palladium, cobalt in the battery, and rare metals in the speakers and screen. A tonne of old phones holds much more gold than a tonne of ore from a typical [[@gold-where|gold mine]], where miners might get just a few grams.

{{story:gold-where}}

For the Tokyo 2020 [[@olympics|Olympics]], held in 2021, Japan asked people to donate old phones and electronics. Over about two years, the public handed in around 79,000 tonnes of devices, including about 6 million phones. All roughly 5,000 gold, silver and bronze medals were made from metal recovered from them.

Every NavIC satellite carries atomic clocks, which keep time to within a few billionths of a second. Each satellite keeps broadcasting the exact time and its own position.

A receiver on the ground hears these signals a tiny moment after they were sent. Radio travels at the speed of light, so a delay of one millionth of a second means the satellite is 300 metres further away. Measuring the delay from several satellites tells the receiver how far it is from each one. With four, it can work out its position, height and the exact time. The maths is the same as with [[@gps-how|GPS]], only with different satellites.

{{story:gps-how}}

Ahmedabad's mosques mix Islamic, Hindu and Jain styles, because local craftsmen built them. The most famous carving is at the Sidi Saiyyed mosque, built in 1573. Its stone windows, called jali, show a tree with curling branches, cut from stone so finely that it looks like lace. The design has become a symbol of the city.

Near Ahmedabad is the [[@india-wonder-rani-ki-vav|Adalaj stepwell]], built in 1499. Steps lead several storeys down to cool water, past carved pillars and walls. Stepwells kept people supplied with water through the hot, dry months, and gave them a shady place to rest. Down at the bottom, the air can feel several degrees cooler than at the top.

{{story:salt-march}}

The rubber fig is a cousin of the [[@banyan|banyan]], and like the banyan it grows [[aerial-root|aerial roots]], roots that hang in the air before reaching the ground. Young roots are soft and bendy. Builders lay them along guides of bamboo or split areca palm stems laid across the stream, and gently tie them in place.

Where two roots press together, they slowly fuse into one. Scientists call this inosculation. Over decades, the roots form a thick net that can carry dozens of people at once. Some bridges stretch more than 20 m, longer than a cricket pitch.

{{story:bridges}}

The busiest lines have separate tracks for slow trains, which stop at every station, and fast trains, which skip many. A board on each platform shows which is coming and where it will stop. Commuters learn to read these instantly.

Stops are short, often under 30 seconds. Passengers start getting off before the train has fully stopped, and others climb on before everyone is out. Regulars know which coach will stop near the exit at their station. There are coaches only for women, and some for people with disabilities and older passengers.

{{story:lunchbox-dabbawala}}

Krishna tells Arjuna that the true self, the soul, is never born and never dies. Bodies change, like clothes, but the self goes on. He urges Arjuna to do his duty, or dharma, as a warrior.

One of the best-known ideas is about action. Krishna says people should do their work well without being attached to the results, whether success or failure. He describes several paths to God: selfless action (karma yoga), knowledge (jnana yoga) and loving devotion ([[bhakti|bhakti]] yoga). Near the end, Krishna shows Arjuna a vast cosmic form, filling all of space and time.

{{story:mahabharata}}

Writing can be a big hurdle. Mandarin uses [[@chinese-characters|characters]], each standing for a word or part of a word. A reader needs to know a few thousand to read a newspaper. Japanese uses three scripts at once, including two sets of sound symbols and thousands of characters borrowed from Chinese.

Sounds matter too. Mandarin has tones: the [[syllable|syllable]] "ma" can mean mother, horse or to scold, depending on whether your voice rises, falls or dips. Some languages of southern Africa use click sounds. Arabic has sounds made deep in the throat that English speakers find tricky.

{{story:bilingual-brain}}
